quick_facts: Bred from uranium-238 inside every uranium-fueled reactor
quick_facts: Half-life: 24,100 years
quick_facts: Bare critical mass: only about 10 kg
quick_facts: Recycled with uranium into MOX fuel rods
quick_facts: Discovered by Seaborg's team in 1941

sources:
    {"organization":"World Nuclear","title":"Plutonium","url":"https://world-nuclear.org/information-library/nuclear-fuel-cycle/fuel-recycling/plutonium"}

definition:
    A man-made fissile metal: reactors breed it when uranium-238 captures a neutron and decays twice through neptunium. With a 24,100-year half-life and a bare critical mass near 10 kg, it powers both warheads and recycled reactor fuel (MOX). Chemically unlike anything natural — warm to the touch from its own decay heat — it was discovered by Glenn Seaborg's team in 1941 and sits at the center of both energy policy and non-proliferation politics.

debunking_snippet:
    “Plutonium is the most toxic substance known.” Folklore. It's radiologically hazardous if inhaled and chemically like other heavy metals — but botulinum toxin and ricin are vastly more poisonous gram-for-gram.
